1	<!doctype html><meta charset=utf-8><meta name=viewport content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
2	<title>Sakia — Ledger of the Lake</title>
3	<style>
4	body{margin:0;background:#14110f;color:#d8cdb8;font-family:Georgia,serif}
5	.top{max-width:640px;margin:0 auto;padding:12vh 1.2em 6vh}
6	h1{font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:.2em;font-size:1.1em;margin:0 0 2em;color:#e8c46a}
7	p{line-height:1.6}small,.m{color:#9a8f7a;font-size:.85em}
8	button{background:none;border:1px solid #e8c46a;color:#e8c46a;padding:.6em 1.4em;font:inherit;cursor:pointer}
9	#hud{position:fixed;right:1em;top:1em;font:14px/1.4 Segoe UI,sans-serif;color:#cfc4ad;text-align:right;background:#14110fcc;padding:.3em .6em;display:none}
10	#yr{font-size:28px;color:#fff}
11	.led{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 .5em}.led img{width:100%;display:block}
12	.end{max-width:640px;margin:0 auto;padding:6vh 1.2em 20vh}
13	</style>
14	<div id=hud><div id=yr>1900</div><div id=mode></div></div>
15	<div class=top><h1>SAKIA</h1>
16	<p>Scroll down. The water comes up and the sound goes under it.</p>
17	<p>Each cream thread is a thousand people. <small>About 100,000 were moved by the High Dam (sources say 100,000&ndash;120,000; I took the lower). The squeezing up the bank before 1964 is my estimate. Temple positions are shape, not measurement. The house colours are from memory; no photographs have reached me.</small></p>
18	<p><button id=go>Begin, with sound</button> <small>&nbsp;or just scroll, in silence</small></p></div>
19	<div class=led><img src=ledger2.png alt="A knitted ledger of Lower Nubia, 1900 to 1980: coloured house-front columns drowned in steps by dark water, gold monuments carried out, cream threads of people crowded onto the shrinking bank, a red stitch at the end."></div>
20	<div class=end><p>Gold: the monuments carried out. The cream dots at the right are the people arriving, each in the row it left. Red: the bag.</p>
21	<p class=m>Wheel in 6/8 over Em&ndash;Bm7. The names in the melody are Meroitic, scanned as syllables; I can say them and I can't read them. The last high note is not under water.</p></div>
22	<div class=end style="padding-top:0"><img src=augustus.png style="width:100%;max-width:520px"><p class=m>Augustus, under the steps where the Meroites put him so they would walk on his face. Made with a machine&rsquo;s brush; the eyes came out turquoise, not calcite, and he looks frightened. I kept that.</p></div>
23	<div class=end style="padding-top:0"><h1 style="font-size:.9em">THE WHEEL SAYS</h1><p>I lift. That is all of it.<br>Water comes up in clay and goes out in a ditch<br>and the ditch is green by evening.</p><p>The ox does not know he is a sentence.<br>The boy on the ox does not know either,<br>and sings so as not to hear me.</p><p>I creak on the same note each turn.<br>That is not grief. That is how a wheel<br>says still here, with the whole of its wood.</p><p>When the water rose to the sill<br>I went on lifting. It was the same water.<br>Nobody told me which side of the river was the river.</p><p>Somebody carried the song out in a suitcase<br>and I am in it, the creak, the long vowel,<br>a thing that turns in the mouth and gets nothing wet.</p><p>Ask me what I lifted. Water.<br>Ask me what it was for. The green.<br>Ask me where the green went.<br>Ask the one who walks on the ditch.</p></div>
24	<div class=end style="padding-top:0"><img src=ditch.png style="width:100%" alt="A green ditch running to the horizon at evening, a figure standing on its bank, a waterwheel far off."><p class=m>No numbers in this one. A small red thing is on the far bank, once.</p></div>
25	<div class=end style="padding-top:0;padding-bottom:4vh"><img src=legs.png style="width:100%;max-width:520px" alt="A broken stone: two robed figures cut off at the break, only hems and legs left, above forty-five rows of invented signs, the last row stopping mid-line."><p class=m>After the Hamadab stela: forty-five rows, a picture above them that is lost. The signs are invented and say nothing; the two hems and four legs are all I could bring myself to put back.</p><h1 style="font-size:.9em;margin-top:2em">THE LEGS SPEAK</h1><p>We were a procession once. Now we are four legs<br>and a hem, and above us the stone has let go<br>of everything that knew where we were going.</p><p>Under us, forty-five rows of people<br>saying what we were walking toward.<br>You can sound them. Nobody can tell us.</p><p>Do not finish us. A head would be a guess<br>and we have been guessed at enough.<br>We are standing the way you stand<br>when you have been told to wait.</p></div>
26	<div class=end style="padding-top:0"><img src=doubloon.png style="width:100%" alt="A gold doubloon stamped with three peaks, a zodiac arch and a sun, ringed by 135 tick-wedges for the chapters of Moby-Dick, seven of them gold."><p class=m>The doubloon nailed to the mast of the Pequod is named in 7 of Moby-Dick's 135 chapters (chapters 9, 16, 32 and 91 also say coin, doubloon or ounce, of other money, and I left them grey), and in one of them (99) twenty-seven times. Nobody spends it. Pip's reading, in that chapter, is the only one I believe: <i>I look, you look, he looks; we look, ye look, they look.</i> The coin's face is drawn from Melville's description, not from a coin.</p><h1 style="font-size:.9em;margin-top:2em">WHAT PIP SAID</h1><p>Starbuck read a valley with a sun in it.<br>Stubb read a year, and was jolly.<br>Flask read sixteen dollars, or nine hundred and sixty cigars.<br>Queequeg held it against his own tattoo.<br>Ahab read Ahab, three times, in three mountains.</p><p>Pip stood in front of it and conjugated.</p><p><i>I look, you look, he looks.<br>We look, ye look, they look.</i></p><p>He is the only one who did not take it down off the mast<br>by being clever. He left it nailed.<br>He read it as the one thing on the ship<br>that every person on it was doing at once.</p></div>
27	<script>
28	const img=document.querySelector('.led img'),yr=document.getElementById('yr'),mode=document.getElementById('mode');
29	function ext(y){return y<1902?0:y<1912?.16:y<1934?.3:y<1964?.42:Math.min(1,.42+.58*(y-1964)/12)}
30	function depth(){const r=img.getBoundingClientRect(),H=r.height,top=r.top;
31	 // image rows 90..(90+243*10) of 2690 are the grid
32	 const y=(innerHeight*.5-top)/H*2690;const f=(y-90)/2430;return Math.max(0,Math.min(1,f))}
33	let ac,lp,wg,lt,letterDone=false,buf={};
34	async function load(n){const r=await fetch(n);return ac.decodeAudioData(await r.arrayBuffer())}
35	function loop(b,dest,g){const s=ac.createBufferSource();s.buffer=b;s.loop=true;const gn=ac.createGain();gn.gain.value=g;s.connect(gn).connect(dest);s.start();return gn}
36	document.getElementById('go').onclick=async e=>{e.target.disabled=true;e.target.textContent='...';
37	 ac=new (window.AudioContext||webkitAudioContext)();lp=ac.createBiquadFilter();lp.type='lowpass';lp.Q.value=.7;lp.frequency.value=16000;
38	 const m=ac.createGain();m.gain.value=.8;lp.connect(m).connect(ac.destination);
39	 const [w,wa,le]=await Promise.all([load('wheel_dry.wav'),load('water.wav'),load('letter.wav')]);buf.le=le;
40	 loop(w,lp,1);const wd=ac.createGain();wd.gain.value=0;wd.connect(ac.destination);wg=wd;loop(wa,wd,.5);
41	 e.target.textContent='the wheel is turning';tick()};
42	function tick(){const f=depth(),y=Math.round(1900+f*80);yr.textContent=y;
43	 const e=ext(y);mode.textContent=y>=1964&&y<1977?'the High Dam fills':'';
44	 document.getElementById('hud').style.display=f>0.001?'block':'none';
45	 if(ac){const c=16000*Math.pow(260/16000,Math.min(1,e*1.15));lp.frequency.setTargetAtTime(c,ac.currentTime,.3);wg.gain.setTargetAtTime(Math.max(0,e-.3)*1.2,ac.currentTime,.5);
46	  if(f>.985&&!letterDone){letterDone=true;const s=ac.createBufferSource();s.buffer=buf.le;s.connect(ac.destination);s.start()}
47	  if(f<.9)letterDone=false}}
48	addEventListener('scroll',()=>{tick()},{passive:true});addEventListener('resize',tick);tick();
49	</script>
